first of all i feel like i have to tell  you i was watching this movie over the  weekend and it started thunderstorming  outside which i feel like just led the  perfect ambiance to the whole experience  so that's perfect i mean that's the best  way to watch a horror film on a rainy  night right but i feel like it really  for me kind of enhanced the experience  because this movie feels like something  i can really only describe as like a  modern gothic  um it definitely has that sensibility  but i would love to know what drew you  to this project initially was it reading  the script did you realize you wanted to  be involved at some other point like  what was the draw for you  i mean look i've been wanting to make a  horror film since uh the day i decided  to becoming a filmmaker which was when i  was 12 years old um i grew up watching  you know  jaws and alien and the omen and psycho  and the birds and i i've always been  drawn to this genre um and so this was  just a dream come true to be able to  tell a horror story but also i love  vampires and when i read blair butler's  script and it was saw that it was a  modern origin story of one of the brides  of dracula i was just like oh sold you  know i hadn't seen that before it felt  fresh like you said it's contemporary  but it's also gothic it's got you know  throwbacks to the old kind of world but  also some things that are really modern  um and also to be honest it was evie's  character she's this 24 year old 26 year  old artist living in new york uh  struggling to get by and i moved to new  york when i was 24 and didn't know a  soul in the world to become a filmmaker  and um i thought that was a character  that was really identifiable and  sometimes you can feel lonely in a big  city and she's really craving human  connection and and that's kind of where  the story goes awry  one of the things i definitely wanted to  ask you about was hiring a female-led  creative team  why was that important to you and what  were the ways in which having that  perspective on this project really made  an impact yeah i mean it's wildly  important to me i  hired my first cinematographer autumn  aiken who uh worked on the light of the  moon my first feature with me  um and all of our creative hods had the  department on set were women and to me  it's just like a difference of  perspective when you get something told  time and time again it's not actually  just like a man versus woman thing it's  like  why do i want to watch the same film  made by the same kind of people as soon  as you add diversity to the mix whether  that's uh you know race religion  background sexuality gender whatever it  is uh you're going to get a more  interesting film just because it hasn't  been done before um and so yeah simply i  wanted to keep it fresh and if you and  you want to do that and also we need  more women in the business so of course  i'm going to hire women  i have to ask you about where this was  filmed because the setting is gorgeous  but really lends a sense of place to the  story what were you most excited about  in getting to film on location and where  did you film specifically just for  people that might not know yeah so we  filmed in hungary um for six months and  we were obviously having hungry  uh stand in the place of new york and in  end of countryside england and so  finding the right style and the right  architectural style was really important  and we got this backstage tour of all  these beautiful castles throughout  hungary which was just for someone who's  like a history buff was just a delight  but when i walked up to this castle in  madison the one that we used in the film  it was in the tudor english style i was  like oh this is and it was so gothic i  just thought oh this is it but then when  i found out that the person who who  built the castle was the great great  grandson of madame barkery who if you  don't know was called countess dracula  or the blood contest because she used to  bathe in the blood of virgins i was like  oh we have to we have to film in this  castle like there's no other choice um  but it was really great to be in eastern  europe where um vlad the impaler for  those who don't know is  arguably the inspiration for dracula um  you know i got to visit the car uh the  prison where he was held prisoner for 13  years the real vibe impaler you know so  just being surrounded by all that  history was just you know wonderful one  of the ways  that this movie i think really succeeds  is how it kind of weaves beauty and  horror together and i think there's a  lot of ways in which it does it and what  the people might not be aware of but the  production design especially i think you  get a really good sense of how that's  subtly woven together so how did you  kind of try to hide those visuals in  plain sight like where people might not  be expecting them yeah thank you for  noticing um it was definitely at the  forefront of my mind um  so to me it's like when you look at that  beautiful banquet scene when you look at  that rehearsal dinner scene you see all  these decadent food and it's all like  beautiful and colorful and kind of  ornate but when you get closer you  realize it's all rotting underneath it's  all dead it's all falling apart there's  even flies and magnets growing in it so  to me it was about creating that  contrast like you said that beauty and  that horror that romance and that horror  that really goes together between the  upstairs and the downstairs world that's  a clear distinction you know the surface  life of these people is all beautiful  and decadent and sumptuous and when you  go downstairs it's kind of rotting in  the underbelly um so we tried to create  that distinction with light with  production design um with color  uh and that was really fun to play with  and i think like you said it's subtle  and it's something that i don't  necessarily want the audience to know  immediately but i'm glad that you  noticed i'm glad that you brought up the  dinner scene because i feel like there  are genuinely scary moments in this  movie but the dinner scene i think is  unsettling in ways that you can't really  i don't want to spoil until people have  seen it but it's it's a lot um how tough  was that to pull off and and how much  advanced prep did you kind of have to  take in in terms of making that scene  happen yeah that was probably the  hardest thing to film it was um other  than the wedding scene which maybe i  shouldn't mention either but like it's  uh um it both took three days to film  and uh you know i remember peter jackson  saying that one of the hardest films to  see  a sense to film for the lord of the  rings was the dinner scene and i was  like that makes no sense then i realized  oh it's because there's so many angers  and so many looks and so many people to  capture when you're sitting around on a  dining table so there's that first of  all the physical challenge of it but  it's also obviously what happens during  that scene and making sure that everyone  is present everyone is you know ready to  deliver that um there's prosthetics  involved uh there's a lot of different  you know there's cranes involved there's  you know a lot of different things going  on in that scene as you pointed out  um and getting that to all come together  is so difficult but also so joyous  because when you see your crew and your  cast and everyone coming together to  work seamlessly and perfectly to pull  off this impossible shot or this  impossible scene it's just such a and  when you see that it works and you feel  that it works um as a filmmaker that's  just the pinnacle i feel like i have to  ask you about working with natalie and  thomas as your leads i mean the movie  hinges on you know their chemistry how  they work together  i i would just love to know what it was  like to work with them to direct them  you know how collaborative they were  because it really does feel like it's  their movie you know it's evie's movie  but it's also you know walters movie and  they're kind of stories that merge for  the first time absolutely i mean natalie  and thomas are a delight to work with i  could not have asked for a more  uh supportive talented friendly  wonderful number one and number two um  and you know i'm someone who really is a  character an actor focused director and  i whatever i am told i am given in  rehearsal time i always double it and  rehearsal time isn't to me isn't about  going over the scripts and things like  that they can know their lines they can  learn that that's that's their jobs but  it's really about diving into the  backstory of the characters getting to  know each other getting to trust each  other quite often i do these exercises  where i ask 30 questions in while  they're in character um and we all and  we all listen and we all kind of  discover the character while we're in  this room um and that builds trust and  and they build their trust in me they  trust in each other and they respect for  each other um and that's what's really  wildly important um and natalie and  thomas just had each other's back i  could tell that they were such good  friends and still are and um you know it  they made my job easy you know and it  also helps that they're both wildly  attractive  and easy on the eye you know that that  makes chemistry  i think you can see it in the finished  product for sure um i am all at a time  but thank you so much for taking the  time to talk with me about this movie it  is so fun and romantic and sexy and  scary and i i'm really excited for  everyone else to get to see it too so  thank you

